  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "How we size a heat pump for a multi-generational Brampton home",
   "html": "<p>Heat pump sizing in Brampton has a twist many installers miss: the number of people. A heat-loss calculation on a 2,200 square foot house gives one answer. Add six adults, three televisions, a second kitchen in the basement and showers running from 6 am to midnight, and the cooling load climbs while the heating load barely moves. Size for winter alone and the summer humidity wins. Size for summer alone and the unit short cycles in January.</p>"
           "<p>An inverter-driven cold-climate heat pump eases most of that tension, because it can idle along at a fraction of its output on a mild day and run flat out at -15°C. We still have to pick the right tonnage, and these are the checks that decide it:</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Heat loss per room, with the finished basement counted as living space</li><li>Internal gains from occupants, cooking and appliances</li><li>Whether the suite gets its own ductless unit, which takes its load off the main system</li><li>Airflow the existing ducts can deliver per ton</li><li>Capacity of the chosen model at -15°C, not just its nameplate rating</li></ul>"
           "<p>The result is a system that is sometimes smaller than the old AC and nearly always quieter. If your household is spread over three floors and a suite, our <a href=\"/brampton/\">Brampton HVAC page</a> explains how zoning and separate systems fit together, and the <a href=\"/heat-pump/cold-climate/\">cold-climate heat pump</a> page covers ratings and backup options for homes across Ontario.</p>"},
